  "narrative": {
    "intro": "This ranking shows federal contract obligations by state for FY2027 to date. The nationwide total of recorded obligations stands at $4.7B. Virginia leads the list with $1.3B in obligations. Texas follows with $410M and California with $399M. Illinois recorded $245M, and both Florida and Maryland recorded $180M each. North Carolina posted $153M, New York posted $130M, the District Of Columbia posted $129M, and Tennessee posted $119M. These figures are partial because they cover the fiscal year to date rather than a complete fiscal year. Contract obligations reflect funds that agencies committed during the reporting period, not necessarily cash outlays. Readers focused on regional planning or business development can use the state-level dollar amounts to identify where federal procurement activity is concentrated so far in FY2027.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which states lead in federal contract obligations?",
        "body": "Virginia is the top state with $1.3B in obligations. Texas is second with $410M and California is third with $399M. Illinois, Florida, and Maryland follow with $245M, $180M, and $180M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are obligations among the top states?",
        "body": "Ten jurisdictions are listed with obligations ranging from $1.3B to $119M. Virginia stands out at $1.3B while the other nine range between $410M and $119M. The data cover FY2027 to date, so these totals are partial for the fiscal year."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Which state gets the most federal contract money?",
      "a": "Virginia receives the most federal contract money. Virginia recorded $1.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date."
    }
